#
# Give an already-configured node the marker it predates.
#
# ⚠️ This file lives in easymesh-wifi, next to the gates that read it, and that
# placement is the whole point.
#
# It used to ship in easymesh-config. base-files default_postinst runs a
# package's uci-defaults first and only then `start` on its init scripts - but
# that ordering holds WITHIN one package. easymesh-config depends on
# easymesh-wifi, so apk configures easymesh-wifi first: its gated services were
# asked to start while the file that writes their answer was still one package
# away. And a procd `start` whose start_service declares no instance does not
# decline - rc.common closes the service and procd drops what was running. So
# every upgrade of the existing fleet had a window in which this package told
# its own services to stop, on a node carrying mesh traffic.
#
# Moving the file removes the window instead of papering over it with a cleverer
# test. There is no heuristic here that the factory cannot reproduce, because
# there no longer needs to be one.
#
# Everything that asks "has anyone made this box a mesh node?" reads
# /etc/mapc/role, written by easymesh-role before it touches anything else.
# Nodes configured before that file existed do not have it, and there has to be
# a way for them to say so - otherwise the gates would treat a working mesh node
# as factory-fresh and stop rebuilding its configuration at boot.
#
# The mark used for that until 2026-08-17 was a non-empty dhcp.lan.ignore. It
# cannot be used, and the reason is worth writing down because three separate
# gates were built on it: easymesh-dhcp-safe writes dhcp.lan.ignore='1' on every
# box that is not a controller, factory ones included. So the mark is set on a
# router nobody has touched, one boot later at the latest, and from then on every
# gate reading it concludes the box is a mesh node. Traced on hardware that day:
# the board came up with map-agent's own fronthaul name across all three radios
# and a backhaul on the air, three builds in a row.
#
# What cannot be faked is a backhaul key. The factory ships the fallback from
# 99-mapcntlr ('1234567890', derived from an empty get_mac_label); a node that
# has joined a mesh carries the controller's real one. A controller is even
# plainer: easymesh-role is the only thing that ever writes dhcp.lan.ignore='0',
# and it writes it together with mapcontroller.controller.enabled='1'.
#
# Runs before 89-easymesh-quiet-until-role, which needs the answer.

[ -f /etc/mapc/role ] && exit 0

# The test itself lives in boot-window.sh, because the gates in easymesh-wifi
# need the same answer during the window before this file has run - see
# is_mesh_node() there. Sourcing it must fail loudly: a silent failure here
# leaves the mark unwritten and every gate answering "factory" on a node that
# is carrying mesh traffic. Exiting non-zero keeps the file for the next boot.
[ -r /usr/share/easymesh/boot-window.sh ] || {
	echo "88-easymesh-role-marker: cannot read boot-window.sh - not marking" >&2
	exit 1
}
. /usr/share/easymesh/boot-window.sh

role=$(configured_mesh_role)
[ -n "$role" ] || exit 0

mkdir -p /etc/mapc 2>/dev/null
echo "$role" > /etc/mapc/role 2>/dev/null
logger -t easymesh-config "configured before /etc/mapc/role existed - marked as $role"
exit 0
